          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01L8naRTiKk9d7BTQ5XUhaDW",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse lexical and dense vector search to improve information retrieval quality.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Corroborated from production systems; single primary source on the source content itself.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines **sparse lexical search** (e.g., BM25) with **dense vector search**, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Components\\n\\n- **Sparse lexical search** (BM25): Efficient exact and substring matching; strong on rare identifiers like error codes, product SKUs, or technical terms\\n- **Dense vector search**: Semantic similarity via learned embeddings; captures paraphrases and conceptually related content\\n- **Merging strategy**: Typically reciprocal rank fusion, which combines rankings from both methods without requiring score normalization\\n\\n### Advantages\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, SKUs, unique technical terms)\\n- Natural language intent (open-ended user questions)\\n\\n### Status\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the **default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems**.\\n\u0022}"
